A||N||7||Monkeyman||Upgrading my laptop...maybe||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||10-10-2000||07:20 PM||||I have an IBM ThinkPad i-series laptop (just bought it used, but in new condition).  Since I'm never satisfied with "stock", I thought I might upgrade it.  Here are some of the specs:  433 Celery processor, 32megs of SDRAM, and a tiny 4.3Gig HD.  IBM says that adding another DIMM is no problem (probably go with an extra 64megs).  It already has a slightly faster processor (400 Celery on the PC).  The problem is the HD.  IBM says that this models HD is not removable/upgradable.  Is there any way to upgrade this without going to anything external?  Words I've heard, but know nothing about are partitioning and FAT32.  What are they and will they do anything for me?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fearless Freep||10-10-2000||08:27 PM||||Partitioning is a way to split your harddrive into 2 logical drive units. (ie. making the computer think there are two drive installed.)  Being that you only have a 4.3...there will be little benefits for you to worry about partitioning.  Partitioning is also a good way to keep the file system away from installed programs.  In NT and NT2000 Servers, you could create a small primary partition for the operating system and jam all the programs on the other (this is a good security measure as you can manipulate the folder security etc.)<P>(layman terms) As for FAT32, this is a way in which the files are stored and used on the harddrive.  regular FAT (File Allocation Table) drives use for just an example say 16KB of space for a single file, by using a FAT32 table, you may only use 8KB per file!  So you figure if your 4.3 is a FAT partition and you only have 200meg left available, if you were to convert to a FAT32 on the same drive you might have 500meg left with the same amount of programs installed. <P>The best way to get to FAT32 will be to simply run have Windows98SE (Second Edition) to have it convert the file table.  If your laptop does not have this option, then it might be just as easy to create a boot/install diskette that has the "fdisk" and format commands from windows98 and do a fresh installation of Windows. In general though, if you have Win98 SE, then 99% of the time, the drive will already be FAT32.<P>A good test would be to drop into dos mode and run the command "fdisk" and then take the option to view the information.  Do not delete the partition information or you will loose your disk.  You only delete the partition information when you are going to be re-formatting the harddrive.<P>Side note, the fdisk command will also be where you create an extra partition.  With windows 98, you can only do this during a fresh drive installation.  With windowsNT and NT2000 systems, you can add and remove partitions all day long so long as you are not converting between the different fat tables (that in windows is a one time deal, once you convert a FAT table to NTFS table you cant revert it)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>Hopefully I havent confused the shi* out of you.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||10-10-2000||09:20 PM||||Let me start by saying that it's a load of IBM-<B>BS</B> that the harddisk can not be replace in an IBM Thinkpad i-series. Other ThinkPad laptops simply had a "user-replaceable" harddisk that didn't involve opening up the laptop itself. For this model, you'll have to open the laptop but after that, the HD can be easily replaced.<P>As for partitioning, that's indeed a way to split one <B>physical</B> harddisk in multiple <B>logical</B> harddisks (for instance, it enables you to create both a drive C: and drive D: on one harddisk). This doesn't help speeding up the HD.<P>As for converting to FAT32: this will enable you to store more files on your harddisk since files will take up less physical space. Indeed, with the old FAT system, a file was stored in blocks of 32K. So a file that was actualy only 2 bytes in size, would take up 32K. Or a 40K file would take up 64K. With FAT32, blocks are reduced to 8K.<P>However, FAT32 will slow down your harddisk a little bit.<P>There are several things to do to speed up your PC (under Windows):<P><UL TYPE=SQUARE><LI>First, get more memory. Windows98 crawls with only 32Mb. You'll need <B>at least</B> 64Mb, but preferrably 128Mb.<BR><LI>Go to Start -&gt; Settings -&gt; Control Panel -&gt; System -&gt; Device Manager -&gt; Harddisks. You'll see something like "GENERIC IDE DISK TYPE 47". That's your harddisk. Double click it and make sure <B>DMA</B> is checked. Ignore the "this might be unsafe" message Windows will pop-up. It's never safe to do something in Windows...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Reboot your computer.<BR><LI>Lower the amount of RAM Windows uses for disk-caching. Windows manages its disk-cache terribly. It will use all memory for its disk-cache leaving none for your applications. Disk-cache memory is hardly ever released causing your machine to continuously swap application memory to the harddisk. To set the cache-size: Start -&gt; Run -&gt; msconfig (OK) -&gt; System.ini. Click on the <B>+</B> sign next to "<B>vcache</B>". If you see "MinFileCache" and "MaxFileCache", then the cache is already restricted and the only thing you might want to do is change the values. By default, these variables are not present and you'll need to add them. To add them, right click on <B>vcache</B> and choose <B>New</B>. In the box that appears, type: <B><I>MinFileCache=4096</I></B> (case sensitive) and press Enter. Right click on <B>vcache</B> again, choose <B>New</B> and type: <B><I>MaxFileCache=24576</I></B>. This last value is dependent of your installed memory. If you have 32Mb installed, type <B><I>MaxFileCache=12288</I></B> instead. For 64Mb or 128Mb, use the first value. Reboot your machine.<BR><LI>Check for unnecessary background tasks. Not all background tasks appear in your systray (the small icons at the bottom right of your screen). Especially Microsoft Office installs a few tasks that slows down your system and that you'll probably never need. One of those tasks is <B>FindFast</B>. To get rid of this: Start -&gt; Run -&gt; msconfig (OK) -&gt; StartUp. See if you can find <B>FindFast</B> and disable it. Reboot your machine.<BR><LI>Change the "primary function" of your computer: Start -&gt; Settings -&gt; Control Panel -&gt; System -&gt; Performance. Click on the <B>File System</B> button. Change "Primary function of this computer" to <B><I>Networkserver</I></B>. The only thing this does, is that Windows will allocate more buffers which will speed up your machine a bit. Reboot your machine.<BR></UL><P>There are lots and lots more tweaks, but it's getting a little late for me here (3am) and my brain is starting to go into <B>Suspend</B>-mode.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||10-10-2000||09:32 PM||||Monkeyman,<BR>If you have a 4.3 GB hard disk, your laptop is probably already set up for FAT 32. Regular FAT (or FAT 16) will only recognize 2 GB. To find out, open "My Computer". Right click on "C". Click "properties". Look for the words "File System". It will either say FAT, or FAT 32.<BR>I also have to agree with Cliff. I think IBM is feeding you a load about the HD not being upgradeable. I'm sure they'd rather sell you a new laptop.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   <P>Raydar <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-10-2000||11:41 PM||||I fix laptops for a living. They are all crap.<P>First thing to learn about laptops is don't store things you don't need all the time. They are portable. They take a beating. the odds the HD will die are Very high. <P>Don't blame the IBM tech. They can get fired if they tell you to open the case to change the HD on units like that. It can be upgraded, it's just not an official IBM path so they aren't alowed to mention it. Remember, tech support lines are regularly monitored by management.<P>Definatly add memory. That's the number one speed killer.<P>If it's a 4 gb C drive then fat32 is already setup. <P>another thing to do is learn how to use regedit. there are places in the registry that likely need cleaning.<P>There's a freeware called Cacheman that does all the system.ini hacks Cliff listed and then some.<P>consider going into control panel's add/remove programs and uninstallig things you don't need.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||10-10-2000||11:45 PM||||Never having dealt with IBM before (I didn't buy the laptop because of the brand, just the age, price and features) I guess I just assumed that they were on the up and up.  (Aren't they the same for hardware as MS is for software?  Everything has to be <I>IBM</I>-compatible?)  Does anyone know anything about changing the HD?  What size, specs, etc?  How difficult would it be to swap HDs?  According to the owners manual, it's a piece of cake to add memory (which is why I plan on doing it).  I don't want to spend an arm and a leg upgrading the HD, but I sure do want more space.  Until then, I'll try the tips I've gotten so far and see if that helps until I get my extra memory (maybe a couple of weeks?).<P>Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-10-2000||11:51 PM||||Post while I'm editing..... The nerve of some people.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||10-11-2000||12:04 AM||||Ogre--Makes sense since I'm a nervous person. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Where do I get Cacheman?  I'd like to give it a try on the PC 1st since that runs ME and can (supposedly) restore itself to an earlier configuration if I screw up.  The laptop has Win98SE.<P>I've been uninstalling programs for a couple of weeks on the PC, but there are a bunch of programs that have been uninstalled that say that there are bits and pieces left.  I think I could do a search for that and find some of the loose ends, but I don't want to remove them just to find that those parts were needed for something else.  I'm assuming that this applies to the laptop also.<P>Last question for the evening (gotta get up in 4 hours).  The system is registered to the previous owner.  How can I change that so it shows registry to me?  Not a big deal but it might be nice in case it was ever stolen or something.||||reg||1
